#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("Skyline Honeylocust", "Gleditsia triacanthos 'Skycole'", "honeylocust")[
  Skyline Honeylocust is one of the most reliable shade trees for the Front Range. Its feathery compound foliage creates dappled shade that allows grass to thrive beneath it. Thornless and seedless, it tolerates drought, alkaline soils, heat, and air pollution well. Golden-yellow fall color is dramatic and long-lasting. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation.
]

=== Watering

Established trees on drip: target 10–15 gallons per watering event, 2 times per week in peak summer (June–August). If your drip emitters are 1 gph, run the zone for 60–90 minutes per cycle. Reduce to once per week in May, September, and October. In drought or heat over 95°F, add a supplemental hand soak at the drip line. Water to 18–24 inches depth — always water deeply and infrequently rather than shallow and frequent. Taper off in fall; no irrigation needed once temperatures stay below 50°F.

=== Fertilization

Your soil has HIGH phosphorus — use an N-K formula only (no 10-10-10). Apply a slow-release N-K fertilizer (16-0-8 or similar) in early spring. More critically given your soil test: apply a full chelated micronutrient package (Fe, Zn, Cu, B, Mn) in May when the tree is leafed out. Honeylocust is the most chlorosis-prone tree in your landscape — your very low iron and zinc readings make interveinal yellowing highly likely. A second chelated iron/micronutrient drench or foliar spray in late June is strongly recommended for this tree specifically.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Prune in late winter (February–March) while dormant. Maintain the natural vase shape; remove dead, damaged, or crossing branches. Train young trees to a central leader. Avoid heavy pruning — honeylocust is prone to decay in large wounds. Clean pruning tools with 10% bleach solution between cuts.

=== Wildlife Value

Skyline Honeylocust is a seedless variety, so it provides minimal wildlife food value. However, the open, dappled canopy provides nesting cover for collared doves and foraging perches for house finches and red-wing blackbirds. The bark and branches may attract northern flickers probing for insects. Cottontail rabbits occasionally browse young twigs in severe winter conditions. Field mice are generally not attracted to established honeylocust. The light canopy allows for good grass growth beneath it, which benefits ground-feeding birds.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+plant+bug+treatment")[Honeylocust plant bug] causes speckled, distorted foliage in spring — treat with neem oil or pyrethroid spray when nymphs are active (May). #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+pod+gall+midge")[Pod gall midge] creates swollen distorted leaf tips; usually cosmetic only, no treatment required. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+spider+mites+treatment")[Spider mites] flare in hot, dry summers — look for stippled yellowing; treat with horticultural oil or bifenthrin. Iron #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+chlorosis+treatment+Colorado")[chlorosis] (yellowing leaves with green veins) is the most important ongoing concern — address with EDDHA chelated iron annually in May and again in late June.

=== Other Care

Mulch 3 inches deep within the drip line. Wrap young trunks in fall to prevent frost cracking on the southwest side — use paper-based tree wrap (not plastic) and remove in early March. Monitor for broken or hanging branches after summer storms, as this is one of the faster-growing trees in the landscape.
